    "details": "cve@mitre.org reports:\n\n> WsgiDAV is a generic and extendable WebDAV server based on WSGI.\n> WsgiDAV 4.3.3 and prior can allow a WebDAV request path containing an\n> encoded parent-directory segment to escape the configured filesystem\n> share root in a specific path layout. The issue is fixed with version\n> 4.3.4.\n\n> WsgiDAV is a generic and extendable WebDAV server based on WSGI. Prior\n> to 4.3.5, the sample MySQLBrowserProvider in\n> wsgidav/samples/mysql_dav_provider.py concatenates the record key\n> parsed from a request URL directly into SQL WHERE clauses. The\n> affected \\_exists_record_by_primary_key, \\_get_field_by_primary_key,\n> and \\_get_record_by_primary_key methods are part of a shipped example\n> provider that is not enabled by default. An attacker who can access a\n> share explicitly configured with this non-default provider can inject\n> SQL through a normal GET request; anonymously exposed read shares\n> permit a status-code oracle and extraction of arbitrary data reachable\n> by the configured MySQL account. This issue is fixed in version 4.3.5.\n\nThe WsgiDAV project reports:\n\n> When follow_symlinks is false (the default), WsgiDAV is expected to\n> prevent serving content through symbolic links - a request for a\n> symlink returns 403 Forbidden: Symlink support is disabled. However,\n> that guard inspects only the leaf component of the resolved path, and\n> the root-containment check is purely lexical (os.path.abspath, which\n> does not resolve symlinks).\n>\n> As a result, a symlink used as an intermediate directory in the\n> request path is neither blocked by the guard nor caught by the\n> containment check, and the request escapes the configured share root.\n> This allows reading, writing, and deleting files outside the root.\n",
    "id": "FreeBSD-2026-0506",
